Step 1: Containerize the Application (Dockerfile)
Package your Python telemetry app and dependencies into an isolated container.

# Use a lightweight official Python runtime
FROM python:3.11-slim

# Set environment variables
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    APP_HOME=/app

WORKDIR $APP_HOME

# Install system dependencies
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    curl \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Install Python dependencies
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

# Copy application source code
COPY . .

# Run application as non-root user for security
RUN useradd -m appuser && chown -R appuser:appuser /app
USER appuser

# Start the async telemetry poller
CMD ["python", "main.py"]


Step 2: Push Metrics to Splunk HEC in Python (splunk_publisher.py)Send structured JSON events asynchronously to Splunk’s HEC endpoint using aiohttp.Pythonimport aiohttp
import asyncio
import json
import logging
from datetime import datetime, timezone

logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)

class SplunkHECPublisher:
    def __init__(self, splunk_url: str, token: str, index: str = "network_telemetry"):
        # HEC standard endpoint: https://<splunk-host>:8088/services/collector/event
        self.endpoint = f"{splunk_url.rstrip('/')}/services/collector/event"
        self.headers = {
            "Authorization": f"Splunk {token}",
            "Content-Type": "application/json"
        }
        self.index = index

    async def send_event(self, session: aiohttp.ClientSession, device_id: str, metrics: dict):
        """Formats and posts structured JSON metrics to Splunk HEC."""
        payload = {
            "time": int(datetime.now(timezone.utc).timestamp()),
            "host": device_id,
            "source": "netpulse-async-poller",
            "sourcetype": "_json",
            "index": self.index,
            "event": {
                "device_id": device_id,
                "status": metrics.get("status", "UP"),
                "cpu_utilization_pct": metrics.get("cpu", 0.0),
                "memory_utilization_pct": metrics.get("memory", 0.0),
                "interface_flaps": metrics.get("flaps", 0),
                "latency_ms": metrics.get("latency", 0.0)
            }
        }

        try:
            async with session.post(self.endpoint, headers=self.headers, json=payload, ssl=False) as resp:
                if resp.status == 200:
                    logging.info(f" Successfully streamed telemetry for {device_id} to Splunk.")
                else:
                    text = await resp.text()
                    logging.error(f" Splunk HEC error {resp.status}: {text}")
        except Exception as e:
            logging.error(f" Failed connecting to Splunk HEC: {e}")

# Example Usage
async def main():
    SPLUNK_URL = "https://splunk.internal.lab:8088"
    HEC_TOKEN = "12345678-1234-1234-1234-1234567890AB"

    async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session:
        publisher = SplunkHECPublisher(SPLUNK_URL, HEC_TOKEN)
        sample_metrics = {"status": "UP", "cpu": 24.5, "memory": 61.2, "flaps": 0, "latency": 12.4}
        await publisher.send_event(session, "router-core-01", sample_metrics)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    asyncio.run(main())
Step 3: Set Up the GitHub Actions CI/CD Pipeline (.github/workflows/ci-cd.yml)This workflow automatically runs on pull requests and pushes to main. It tests code quality, runs unit tests, builds the Docker image, and validates the Splunk exporter configuration.YAMLname: CI/CD Pipeline & Code Quality Verification

on:
  push:
    branches: [ "main" ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ "main" ]

jobs:
  lint-and-test:
    name: 🧪 Lint & Run Unit Tests
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout Repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Python 3.11
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.11"
          cache: "pip"

      - name: Install Dependencies
        run: |
          python -m pip install --upgrade pip
          pip install flake8 pytest aiohttp
          if [ -f requirements.txt ]; then pip install -r requirements.txt; fi

      - name: Lint with Flake8 (Code Quality)
        run: |
          # Stop build if there are Python syntax errors or undefined names
          flake8 . --count --select=E9,F63,F7,F82 --show-source --statistics
          # Exit-zero treats all errors as warnings
          flake8 . --count --exit-zero --max-complexity=10 --max-line-length=127 --statistics

      - name: Execute Unit Tests (Pytest)
        run: |
          pytest tests/ --doctest-modules --junitxml=junit/test-results.xml

  docker-build:
    name: 🐳 Build Docker Container
    needs: lint-and-test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout Repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Docker Buildx
        uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3

      - name: Build Local Image
        uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
        with:
          context: .
          push: false
          tags: netpulse-telemetry:latest
          cache-from: type=gha
          cache-to: type=gha,mode=max

      - name: Verify Container Image Startup
        run: |
          docker run --rm netpulse-telemetry:latest python -c "print('Docker container build verified successfully!')"
Step 4: Configure Splunk HTTP Event Collector (HEC)To receive data in Splunk:Enable HEC: In Splunk Web, go to Settings $\rightarrow$ Data Inputs $\rightarrow$ HTTP Event Collector $\rightarrow$ Global Settings $\rightarrow$ Enable HTTP Event Collector (Default Port: 8088).Create New Token: Click New Token, name it netpulse-collector, set Source Type to _json, and assign a destination index (e.g., network_telemetry).Save Token: Save the generated GUID token and add it to your environment variables or secret store (SPLUNK_HEC_TOKEN).
